WEBVTT

NOTE
Subtitles for the shared tutorial video (Turing machine demo).
Language: English, from the spoken audio.

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Hello everyone, this is our training center, and I'm
giving a quick overview of how the Turing machine works.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.000
First of all, you need to select the module
Fundamentals of Computer Science.

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.000
Then you have to select the topic of the Turing machines.

4
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Here we can go to the simulation tab and just

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
enter some Turing machine that we want.
For example, one that

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:39.000
can bit-flip certain tape contents,
so that you can

7
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.000
basically invert the tape.

8
00:00:51.000 --> 00:01:01.000
With our states set up, we can enter something on the
tape that then gets inverted.

9
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:08.000
You can just automatically let it play and watch
the Turing machine invert tape contents

10
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.000
until the entire tape is inverted.

11
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:18.000
We can also go back to the configuration
and change stuff around here.

12
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:26.000
For example, add a new state or remove it, or add new
characters that can also do things on our machine.

13
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:33.000
We can also export the whole machine
to this compressed string format.

14
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:39.000
We can copy that compressed string, and when we
come back after doing something else,

15
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.000
we can put the string in, import it,
and our machine is back.

16
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:53.000
We also have the possibility to look at some examples
to get a better grasp of how everything works.

17
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:02.000
For example, we can look at a binary increment —
how you would increment this number by one.

18
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:09.000
There are also exercises you can do, like inverting
a binary word, basically what we just did.

19
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:17.000
Then we can enter a machine, for example what we just
did, and check if the solution is right.

20
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:27.000
So we halt every time — for example, we end in a
halt state, and we pass the test.

21
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:35.000
We can also load a pre-built solution, which is
identical, because I stole that example.

22
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:41.000
And that's it about the Turing machine,
with this short test video.
